\subsection{Related work}
\label{sec:recent-marine-inertial-work}
Marine observers combine inertial sensing, sensor-bias estimation, and
vessel-specific aiding. Virtual vertical references have been used for
GNSS/INS integration and six-degree-of-freedom estimation with MEMS sensors
\cite{Bryne2015_VirtualVerticalReference,Bryne2018_VVRAidedINS,
Bryne2017_INSObserverImplementation,Rogne2021_LowCostMEMSDP}.
Related studies address attitude/heave and adaptive wave filtering in marine
strapdown INS \cite{Bryne2016_AttitudeHeaveMEMS,Bryne2017_AdaptiveWaveINS}.
Kalman approaches include discrete-time heave reconstruction and fusion of
inertial, GNSS, and virtual measurements to separate wave-frequency heave
from slower vessel height \cite{Reis2023_DiscreteKalmanHeave,
Overaas2025_HeightKalman,Li2025_HeaveCKF}.

Invariant and equivariant filters provide alternative joint attitude,
kinematic, and bias formulations
\cite{BarrauBonnabel2017_IEKF,Fornasier2022_EquivariantINSBias,
VanGoor2023_EquivariantINS}. Marine INS/DVL applications and covariance
reparameterization studies further develop these geometric approaches
\cite{Du2024_LieGroupINSDVL,Qian2025_SE33DVL,Maurer2026_LeftRightIEKF}.
OU--III instead retains a conventional quaternion MEKF. Its contribution is
the OU-driven translational chain, integral-state regularization, and
measurement-only sea adaptation, not a new invariant error geometry.
